# Intro
One fixed star in a static, periodic glass box of gas in two phases in
pressure balance: `x < L/2` cold (500 K, `n`, particle mass `m`), `x >= L/2`
hot (8000 K, `n/16`, `m/16`). Both phases share the glass positions and
hence the smoothing length; the sound speed differs by 4, so the CFL time
steps of the two phases sit two time bins apart. The star sits two
interparticle spacings inside the hot phase, its kernel reaching both
phases, on a step longer than its hot neighbours' step.

The fixture exercises the ISRF dose reservoir and the hyperbolic
propagation across a real time-bin hierarchy. With no dust (`Z = 0`) and a
pinned `c_hyp`, the stored energy grows as `(c_hyp/c) L t`, so
`(c/c_hyp) sum m u` tracks `L t` up to the dose still held in the
reservoirs and in the gas steps in flight.

**This example reports, it does not gate the conservation claim.** The lag
`L t - (c/c_hyp) sum m u` is bounded, but it is quantised in whole particle
steps, so it steps between neighbouring integer multiples of the fine step
instead of varying smoothly. A fitted slope reads that bounded quantised
offset as a drift and reports a spurious violation even on a single-bin
control. The check prints the slope and the lag; only the time-bin
structure and the absorption-free setup are gated. A working gate needs a
bounded-window metric on the lag, with the window derived from the measured
bin structure rather than set by hand.

**There is no steady-state comparison, by design.** The box is not
hydrostatic: the hot phase densifies over the run in any time-step
configuration, so comparing radiation fields between a hierarchy run and a
single-bin run measures hydrodynamic drift, not the radiation field. Such a
comparison needs a hydrostatic fixture.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```

# Run
```
./run.sh
```
`run.sh` runs `conservation_hierarchy` and `conservation_single_bin` into
their own directories. Both set `Z = 0` and pin `c_hyp` to 5 km/s, a debug
parameter used here only so that `c/c_hyp` is one number for the whole box.
The single-bin run sets `dt_max` below every CFL step of the box, so all gas
and the star share one bin.

Environment overrides: `n_threads`, `swift` (binary path), `level`
(`N = (2**level)**3`), `gas_density` (cold phase, atom/cm^3), `gas_mass`
(cold phase, Msun), `T_cold` (K, below 625 K so that the hot phase stays
under 1e4 K), `star_mass`, `c_hyp_pin` (km/s), `dt_fine` (`dt_max` of the
single-bin run, slightly above the wanted power-of-two bin, which SWIFT
floors a step to), and `runs`. At a finer `level`, divide `gas_mass` by 8
and `dt_fine` by 2 per level.

# Checking
```
python3 isrf_timestep_hierarchy_injection_check.py [--dir runs_directory]
```
`run.sh` runs it at the end. It exits nonzero if any gate fails; a
non-finite value fails its gate. Gated, per run: partial and all-gas update
steps in the hierarchy run with the cold phase on at least twice the hot
phase's step, all-gas updates on every step in the single-bin run, the star
step longer than the shortest gas step in the hierarchy run, pinned
`c_hyp dt/h` at most `ISRF_c_hyp_margin` in each phase, and zero
metallicity. Reported without a bar, per band: the fitted slope of
`(c/c_hyp) sum m u / L` against time with its standard error, and the lag
`L t - (c/c_hyp) sum m u` in units of each run's own fine step, which is
not the same step in the two runs.
